---
import CalculatorLayout from '@/layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import LogarithmCalculator from '@/components/calculators/LogarithmCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is a logarithm?',
    a: 'A logarithm answers “what power do I raise the base to, to get this number?”. log₁₀(1000) = 3 because 10³ = 1000, and log₂(8) = 3 because 2³ = 8.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between log and ln?',
    a: '“log” usually means base 10 (the common logarithm), used in science and engineering scales. “ln” is the natural logarithm, base e ≈ 2.71828, used in growth, decay and calculus.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I calculate a logarithm in any base?',
    a: 'Use the change-of-base formula: log_b(x) = ln(x) ÷ ln(b), or log(x) ÷ log(b). For log₂(50): ln(50) ÷ ln(2) = 3.912 ÷ 0.693 ≈ 5.644.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you take the log of zero or a negative number?',
    a: 'Not with real numbers. No power of a positive base gives zero or a negative number, so log(0) and log of negatives are undefined.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are logarithms used for?',
    a: 'Measuring things that vary hugely in size: earthquake magnitude, sound in decibels, acidity (pH), star brightness, and working out doubling times and compound interest periods.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="logarithm-calculator"
  title="Logarithm Calculator – log, ln & Any Base"
  description="Free logarithm calculator. Find the logarithm of any number in base 10, base e (ln), base 2 or any base you choose, with the change-of-base working."
  intro="Find the logarithm of any positive number in base 10, base e (natural log), base 2 or any other base, with a check and the change-of-base working."
  faqs={faqs}
  updated="2026-09-27"
>
  <LogarithmCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="learn">
    <h2>How to use the logarithm calculator</h2>
    <p>Enter the number and the base, or tap 10, e or 2. You’ll see the result, a check that the base raised to it gives your number, and the common, natural and binary logs side by side.</p>

    <h2>Logarithm definition and rules</h2>
    <div class="formula">log_b(x) = y means bʸ = x &nbsp;&nbsp; log_b(x) = ln(x) ÷ ln(b)</div>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>log(xy) = log x + log y</td><td>log(20) = log 2 + log 10 ≈ 1.301</td></tr>
        <tr><td>log(x ÷ y) = log x − log y</td><td>log(5) = log 10 − log 2 ≈ 0.699</td></tr>
        <tr><td>log(xⁿ) = n × log x</td><td>log(1000) = 3 × log 10 = 3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>log_b(b) = 1, log_b(1) = 0</td><td>ln(e) = 1, log(1) = 0</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>
    <p>How many times must you double $1 to reach $1,000? That’s log₂(1000) = ln(1000) ÷ ln(2) ≈ 6.908 ÷ 0.693 ≈ <strong>9.97</strong>, so just under 10 doublings.</p>

    <h2>Common values</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>x</th><th>log₁₀</th><th>ln</th><th>log₂</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>0.30103</td><td>0.69315</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10</td><td>1</td><td>2.30259</td><td>3.32193</td></tr>
        <tr><td>100</td><td>2</td><td>4.60517</td><td>6.64386</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1,000</td><td>3</td><td>6.90776</td><td>9.96578</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>To raise numbers to powers, use the <a href="/exponent-calculator">exponent calculator</a>.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
